# Logic is next: It's important to remember that, unlike languages like Python, boolean values cannot be nested (e.g. x = 1 or y = 2) in if functions and therefore require logic to be completed in steps using the bool function.

bool x == 14 or x == 13 # Outputs 1 if x is either 13 or 14. Otherwise it outputs 0. The output is a variable called 'bool.out', or simply '.out' in other bool functions.
bool .out and y == 2 # Outputs 1 if bool.out (defined in the previous function, translatable to "x is either 13 or 14") is true (or 1), and y is equal to 2.
bool .out xor n >= 17 # Outputs 1 to bool.out if bool.out is 1 (which would mean that x is 13 or 14 and y is 2) or n is greater than or equal to 17, but not if both conditions are true.
bool .out nand not n < 18 # Outputs  to bool.out only if bool.out is 1 and n is not less than (or greater than or equal to) 18.
# The final result of this is only 1 (aka True) if:
# - x is 13 or 14
# - y is 2
# - n is less than 18 but greater than or equal to 17
# Or if any one of the outputs is false, except the last one.
